# Dependencies
node_modules/
.pnp
.pnp.js

# Build output — anchored to the repository root on purpose. The entire
# data/ tree is committed (design D1, task 1.3); nothing under data/ may
# ever be ignored, and an unanchored pattern here could do that by accident.
/.next/
/out/
/build/
/dist/

# Static assets generated into public/ by scripts/prebuild.mjs so they land in
# out/ (design D1's rule for /status.json, extended in task 4.9/4.12/4.13).
# Build output like any other: regenerated on every build, never hand-edited.
# The committed copies of the data they derive from live under data/, which is
# never ignored.
/public/status.json
/public/search-index.json
/public/catalog.json
/public/catalog/
/public/feeds/
/public/dataset/
# robots.txt moved out of app/robots.ts so the crawler stance could carry its
# reasoning as comments in the SERVED file — Next's robots generator cannot
# emit any (lib/crawlers.mjs, beads addictedtoai-k1j). llms.txt joined it.
# Generated on every build like everything above, never hand-edited.
/public/robots.txt
/public/llms.txt
# The IndexNow key file (lib/asset-routes.mjs, beads addictedtoai-k1j). Named
# for the key itself, which is what the protocol requires and which is why this
# line carries a literal: ROTATING THE KEY MEANS EDITING THIS LINE TOO.
# `pulse/tests/indexnow.test.mjs` asserts the two agree, so a rotation that
# forgets fails the tests rather than committing build output.
/public/0f53367020cd11b3a49cebd8c5b4d382.txt

# The two brakes (beads addictedtoai-ufu). `STOP` is the maintainer's; `HOLD.md`
# is the loop's self-halt, written when a breaker trips. Both address a person
# at this keyboard — this repository is built and operated on one machine —
# and neither carries repository content, so neither belongs in a commit.
#
# It is ignored rather than remembered because a halt signal is exactly the
# thing a stray `git add -A` should not be able to push to the remote, and
# "don't commit it" was previously an instruction held in one memory entry.
# Guardrails here are mechanisms, not instructions. This one is now structural:
# `HOLD.md` appeared on 2026-08-29 when breaker 2 correctly tripped and sat in
# `git status` as an untracked file while five agents worked in this tree.
#
# Ignoring them does NOT relax any rule about them. Everything that acts on
# these files reads the FILESYSTEM, never git — `loop/lib/breakers.mjs`
# (`startGate`) and `pulse/lib/publish.mjs` both `existsSync` them — so the
# Desk still refuses to start while `HOLD.md` exists, publish is still
# suspended, and removing `HOLD.md` is still the maintainer's alone. Nothing
# commits them either: `pulse/lib/publish.mjs` stages `data content public` by
# name, `loop/run.mjs` stages its records by exact path, and the only `add -A`
# in the machinery runs inside a job worktree.
#
# One thing it DOES change, measured rather than assumed and filed as
# addictedtoai-59q: breaker 4 spotted a job creating `STOP` by finding it in the
# branch diff, and an ignored file never reaches a diff. That detection is gone.
# The harm it detected is gone too, and structurally — a job's `STOP` lives in a
# scratch worktree, and commit-then-merge was its only route to this root.
#
# Anchored, like the build-output patterns above and for the same reason: an
# unanchored `STOP` would swallow any file or directory called STOP anywhere in
# the tree, `content/` included.
/HOLD.md
/STOP

# A job's RESULT.md, for the same reason and by the same argument. It is the
# executor's output channel (loop/lib/result.mjs), read from the worktree and
# never repo content. loop/run.mjs removes it before merging, but that removal
# is an instruction; this is the mechanism, and the two together are what stops
# it reaching main. It landed once — j-20260830-01, the first job ever to merge
# successfully, carried it into the root tracked and pushed, because every
# earlier run had failed before reaching the merge.
/RESULT.md

